A||||24||Cliff Pennock||What's up with my battery?||||1||
Z||000000||Cliff Pennock||12-20-2001||04:42 PM||||About a year ago, I had both the alternator and the battery replaced in my car because the battery would be dead if I left the car for about 4 days. After replacement, I didn't have that problem anymore... until a few weeks ago when it really got cold here. In fact, I'm unable to start the car if I have driven my Fiero the <I>day before</I> with my lights on.<P>But somehow, the symptoms are a bit different. With the old battery and alternator, the battery would be obviously low - the courtesy lights would be dim and if I tried to crank it they would go out. Now, although my voltmeter shows a very low voltage, the lights <I>aren't</I> dim and trying to crank the car doesn't make them any dimmer. I have a portable power station, and when I hook that up, I can start the car fine so it does look like a low battery problem.<P>Also, when I drive with my lights on and I'm at a traffic light, I can see the voltmeter drop in sync with my turn signals. I can't remember if it ever did this before, but that could be a sign of a bad alternator.<P>Anyways, anybody have a clue what this could be? Is my battery and/or alternator bad <I>again</I>?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Loki||12-20-2001||04:50 PM||||Have the battery tested... Sound slike a bad one or a bad connection..ive sold batteries that come back a week later already bad.  Easier to have battery tested than Alt.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fieroloki.com/lokisig1%20copy.jpg">   <BR>"All Fieros broke right now" :(||||reg||1
Z||000002||REDHOTT88||12-20-2001||05:03 PM||||    <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Loki:<BR><B>Have the battery tested... Sound slike a bad one or a bad connection..ive sold batteries that come back a week later already bad.  Easier to have battery tested than Alt.       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>He's right Cliff, I've bought a new battery or two before and had to bring them back. One had a bad cell, the other wouldn't hold a charge. <P>(I'm sure theres someone here that will give you a more detailed technical answer, but a bad battery would be my first suspect... next would be the voltage regulator, do Fiero's have voltage regulators? )<P><BR>------------------<BR>                                                                                                         <BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/FIREdevil.gif">             <FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> <B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed  </FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1">(Runs smooth, shifts crisp, red and shiny all over!)</FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||ray b||12-20-2001||05:43 PM||||Have you got a VOM meter ck the volts both off battery and with it running for the alt output.<BR>Check for bad connections and or grounds too<BR>if battery and alt read good.<BR>coke the posts or wire brush them just to be sure of good contacts on the battery.<BR>check for bad fuses or links if still a mystery voltage drop.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000004||Adrift||12-20-2001||06:31 PM||||This might sound kind of redundent but I had the same problem on my car - what i did was take a the steel brush for cleaning my barbecue and cleaned out the terminal on the battery well, and replaced the two screws which are on the wires which go into the batter (Total cost: $3.00) Went  from not being able to turn over at all (whole car goes dead when attempting to) to turning over each time!||||reg||1
Z||000005||GTDude||12-20-2001||08:13 PM||||Redundant here too......clean your battery Cliff.....sometimes the simplest things are the hardest to figure out!  <P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||12-20-2001||08:49 PM||||a voltmeter across the battery terminals will check both. good battery with engine off will be close to 12 volts. with it running should show between 13.5 - 14.5 volts for alternator charge. Leave the lites on while you check the alt charge, you need the test with a load. Oh and forgot, you might have an internal starter short too.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||jelly2m8||12-20-2001||08:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>a voltmeter across the battery terminals will check both. good battery with engine off will be close to 12 volts. with it running should show between 13.5 - 14.5 volts for alternator charge.<BR>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-20-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually a fully charged battery will show 12.6 volts on the voltmeter. A battery with 12.4 volts is only 75% charged.<P> A battery that reads 12 volts is almost dead.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||gggg-us||12-20-2001||08:58 PM||||A different suggestion... I had a 74 Nova with and elusive battery drain problem, and after chaising my tail with the altenator and battery, and checking and rechecking just about the entire circuit in the car, I found the problem to be a rear tail light/ turn signal night. If the car has signal lights that are two stage...ie two filaments in the light, they can brake in such a way that one filament causes a short to the other, and causes a battery drain. This could be a possible problem, since you mentioned the dimming of lights when your turn signal is on.<P>------------------<BR>88 4Cyl ... Rebuilding 81 Vette Engine for it now.||||reg||1
Z||000009||West Coast Fiero||12-20-2001||09:07 PM||||What is the rating of Cold Cranking Amps ( CCA ) on your battery, and how cold is it there when you try to start<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cliff Pennock||12-20-2001||09:27 PM||||I have no idea what the CCA is (I need to check tomorrow). It's freezing here right now.<P>Like I said, the strange thing is that the lights do not dim when I turn the ignition.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Old Lar||12-20-2001||09:43 PM||||How about the starter?  Turn the key, lights stay bright, starter doesn't crank.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cliff Pennock||12-21-2001||07:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B>How about the starter?  Turn the key, lights stay bright, starter doesn't crank.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I've thought about it being the starter as well because that would explain why the lights stay bright when I try to start the car, but when it does crank it cranks very slow which indicates a low battery. And when I hook up the power station, it cranks fine.||||reg||1
Z||000013||revin||12-21-2001||08:09 AM||||There is a drain somewhere. Have you checked for a short somewhere? Did you install/replace anything recently? Check there. I have heard you can use the meter at the fuse box with the car off and check out each conn for stray volts to help track down a short or open.  <P>------------------<BR>Revin||||reg||1
Z||000014||montage||12-21-2001||08:30 AM||||Cliff: To recap some things that have been mentioned and add my two cents to systemtically troubleshoot I would do the following. Check the voltage of your alternator during full charge with all accesories on, it should be 13.5 to 14.5 (best thing is a amp meter to check charge current). Next remove battery cables and check battery voltage and/or use a hydrometer (couple bucks at car parts, Wallmart, etc.) which is best. If this is good clean all connections (make sure they were all tight) to the battery and go to the other end of positive and ground and clean all of these connections. A dirty connection will cause a voltage drop and draw a large current. If after all of this the starter still is slow (provided your battery has sufficent CCA) then the stator on your starter is probably touching the magnets on the case causing a short and a large and current draw. Usually the bushings go on the stator ends causing it to touch the sides. My 2 cents. Good Luck.<P>Merry Christmas to all.   ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Oreif||12-21-2001||08:49 AM||||If the lights stay bright when cranking, it could also be the connections on the starter could be loose or corroded. This will affect the charging of the battery. Another quick way to see if the alternator is good, is to start the car and remove the positive side of the battery. If car dies, alternator is bad. I had a car that had a bad diode cluster. It would charge the battery very slowly. So if I used lights the night before the car wouldn't start the next day. The alternator was only putting out 9.2V. Barely enough to keep the battery going. <P>I would clean all connections including the ones on the starter then see how the car acts.<P>------------------<BR>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">           <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Cliff Pennock||12-21-2001||09:29 AM||||Thanks guys, as soon as I can find the time, I'll do all those test.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>Another quick way to see if the alternator is good, is to start the car and remove the positive side of the battery.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Just a note of caution to anyone trying this out: before you remove the positive cable, first make sure you have disconnected all sensitive electronic equipment like mobile phone chargers, some radios, etc. Removing the terminal will cause a temporary transient voltage spike of 400V+. Your ECM is protected against that, so are most car radios. But mobile chargers etc. are not. So unless you want stuff exploding left and right in your car, remove them first. Just a little disclaimer.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||Butter||12-21-2001||12:49 PM||||I always disconnect the negative terminal to avoid a dead short in case the wrench slips into a ground.  This way you only have to avoid the opposite terminal.<P>I have experienced charging problems of your nature due to the parts house selling me a lower rated amperage alternator than was suppose to be on the car.  They seem to work alright for a while then after some age just ain't got enough juice to keep things goin'.  Shorted field coils or a bad diode in the bridge rectifier of the alternator can cause the amperage rating to fall off and still charge enough to keep the engine running with the battery post removed.  This is not a likely thing to happen but I have experienced it before and thought I'd offer it if the other good advise doesn't solve your problem.  Personally on charging problems I always start by taking off the battery post and cleaning them with a wire brush.<P>P.S. Make sure you haven't bump on the interior lights<p>[This message has been edited by Butter (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||next level trends prez||12-21-2001||01:11 PM||||i had the sam probablem in one of myother cars i had a 92 geo storm and i drove on it for a year or so like that... if i didnt rive on it for a couple of days it would be dead but if i drove it every day it was ok if i drove it at night sometimes the next day it would be dead but not all the time.... after a i stoped driving it it sat for two months then a friend bought it.. two days later it wouldnt do anything... heres the bad news.. it was the computer... going bad the whole time...cause after we replaced it the turn signals where fine and the batt we put in hasnt had to be charger since then its been three months... the car runs great now... it could very well be your computer...||||reg||1
Z||000019||jelly2m8||12-21-2001||10:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by next level trends prez:<BR><B>i had the sam probablem in one of myother cars i had a 92 geo storm ... heres the bad news.. it was the computer... going bad the whole time...cause after we replaced it the turn signals where fine and the batt we put in hasnt had to be charger since then its been three months... the car runs great now... it could very well be your computer...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR> Nope, the reason the Geo needed an ECM was likely because the voltage regulator is built into the ECM. Chrysler is a big fan of this too.<P> The Fiero, or any other GM product that I'm aware of  has the voltage regulator built in the alternator.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||theogre||12-21-2001||11:21 PM||||Welllll.....<P>It could be any number of things.<P>Get the charging system checked is #1. If that replacement alternator is batty then the battery will be to.<P>Try disconnecting the battery overnight for a couple nights. This will tell you really fast if there's something sucking the life out of it while parked or if there is a partly shorted cell.<P>There are several places where you could have a crappy connection. you've eliminated most of them, and <I>maybe</I> the starter because the porta pack starts it... keep in mind tho... the porta pack increases available power. If you are suffering a voltage drop in the starter of cables then the porta pack may simply be masking it.<P>That means the cables and started still have to be checked.<P>don't forget the thin + wire off the battery.... if that wire is crappy then the battery won't charge right.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000021||next level trends prez||12-22-2001||12:49 PM||||he might be right.. about the geo have the voltege regulator in the ecm.. but it still could be your computer... just try it if it aint it then take the computer back...||||reg||1
Z||000022||theogre||12-22-2001||01:00 PM||||I very much doubt this is an ECM problem.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Sage||12-22-2001||07:33 PM||||I think just about everything has been covered pretty well up to this point, but if your battery, alternator and starter all check good, sounds like you need to find a bad connection, in particular a ground.  The connector block on the passenger side just behind the battery would be suspect and the ground from there to the chassis.  There are several grounds to the block as well that could give you problems.  Theogre suggestion about unhooking the batt. for a couple nights is about the best idea I've seen mentioned, as like he said, this will tell you if something is draining the juice out of it, while the car is sitting.<P>Being a non metal bodied car,  it is subject to ground problems just like the Corvette, which had many ground problems, especially the C3 and C4.  The fact you mentioned that the headlights don't dim when on and cranking the car, kind of further suggests a ground problem.  Could be waaay off base here, but "if the logical is not true, then the illogical must be".   <P>I hate electrical problems!  Hope you get it figured out, post haste!<P>Happy holidays to you and yours.<P>Peace  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Have a good one!||||reg||5
Z||000024||SLYGUY||12-22-2001||09:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B><BR>when I drive with my lights on and I'm at a traffic light, I can see the voltmeter drop in sync with my turn signals. <I>again</I>?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My car does this also. With both a good alternator and battery you should see this. An accurate alternator guage will show both charge AND discharge. Any electrical draw will show up. The idea is to not have any one, or group, of electrical sources produce a draw significant enough to diminish the charge that your alternator is trying to deliver to the battery. <P>If I had that problem I would go through a process of elimination Cliff. First I would have the alternator checked, and replace the battery while I was at it. <BR>I would take a close look at both the connectors to the battery, and the alternator. Since there is a symptom of no crank and the lights do not dim I'd check the connections to the starter. Perhaps yank that and have it checked also.<P>Good luck!<P>Sly<P>||||reg||1
